Article 30 The following medical expenses shall not be included in the payment scope of the basic medical insurance fund:
(1) those that shall be paid from the work-related injury insurance fund;
(2) those that shall be borne by a third party;
(3) those that shall be borne by public health;
(4) those for medical treatment abroad.
Where medical expenses shall be borne by a third party according to law, if the third party does not pay or cannot be determined, the basic medical insurance fund shall pay in advance. After the basic medical insurance fund pays in advance, it has the right to recover from the third party.

Article 37 Injuries or deaths of employees caused by any of the following circumstances during work shall not be identified as work-related injuries:
(1) intentional crime;
(2) drunkenness or drug use;
(3) self-injury or suicide;
(4) other circumstances prescribed by laws and administrative regulations.

Article 38 The following expenses incurred due to work-related injuries shall be paid from the work-related injury insurance fund in accordance with State regulations:
(1) medical expenses and rehabilitation expenses for treating work-related injuries;
(2) hospital meal subsidies;
(3) transportation and accommodation expenses for medical treatment outside the pooling region;
(4) expenses for installing and configuring disability assistive devices;
(5) living care expenses confirmed by the work capacity assessment committee for those who cannot take care of themselves;
(6) one-time disability subsidies and monthly disability allowances for employees with disability levels 1 to 4;
(7) one-time medical subsidies that shall be enjoyed upon termination or dissolution of the labor contract;
(8) funeral subsidies, survivor benefits, and death subsidies for those who die from work-related injuries, to be received by their survivors;
(9) work capacity assessment fees.

Article 39 The following expenses incurred due to work-related injuries shall be paid by the employer in accordance with State regulations:
(1) wages and welfare during the treatment of work-related injuries;
(2) monthly disability allowances for employees with disability levels 5 and 6;
(3) one-time disability employment subsidies that shall be enjoyed upon termination or dissolution of the labor contract.

Article 43 A work-related injured employee shall stop enjoying work-related injury insurance benefits under any of the following circumstances:
(1) losing the conditions for enjoying the benefits;
(2) refusing to undergo work capacity assessment;
(3) refusing treatment.

Article 45 Unemployed persons who meet the following conditions may receive unemployment insurance benefits from the unemployment insurance fund:
(1) the employer and the individual have paid unemployment insurance premiums for at least one year before unemployment;
(2) the employment is interrupted not due to the individual's own will;
(3) the individual has registered for unemployment and has a job-seeking requirement.

Article 51 An unemployed person who, during the period of receiving unemployment insurance benefits, falls under any of the following circumstances, shall cease to receive the unemployment insurance benefits and at the same time cease to enjoy other unemployment insurance benefits:
(1) having re-employed;
(2) having been enlisted for active military service;
(3) having emigrated abroad;
(4) having begun to receive basic old-age insurance benefits;
(5) without justifiable reason, refusing to accept suitable work introduced or training provided by the department or institution designated by the local people's government.

Article 55 Maternity medical expenses include the following items:
(1) medical expenses for childbirth;
(2) medical expenses for family planning;
(3) other itemized expenses as prescribed by laws and regulations.

Article 56 An employee who falls under any of the following circumstances may enjoy maternity allowance as prescribed by the State:
(1) female employees enjoying maternity leave for childbirth;
(2) enjoying leave for family planning surgery;
(3) other circumstances prescribed by laws and regulations.
The maternity allowance shall be calculated and paid according to the average monthly wage of the employees of the employer in the preceding year.

Article 79 The social insurance administrative department shall conduct supervision and inspection over the revenues and expenditures, management, and investment and operation of social insurance funds. If problems are discovered, it shall propose corrective suggestions, make a decision on handling according to law, or propose handling suggestions to the relevant administrative department. The results of the inspection of social insurance funds shall be periodically published to the public.
When the social insurance administrative department conducts supervision and inspection over social insurance funds, it shall have the authority to take the following measures:
(1) to consult, record, and copy materials related to the revenues and expenditures, management, and investment and operation of social insurance funds, and to seal up materials that may be transferred, concealed, or destroyed;
(2) to inquire of the entities and individuals related to the matters under investigation, and require them to provide explanations on issues related to the matters under investigation and to provide relevant evidentiary materials;
(3) to stop acts of concealing, transferring, misappropriating, or diverting social insurance funds, and order rectification.

Article 89 Where a social insurance agency or any of its staff members commits any of the following acts, the social insurance administrative department shall order a correction; if losses are caused to the social insurance fund, the employer, or the individual, the agency shall bear compensation liability according to law; the persons directly in charge and other persons directly responsible shall be given sanctions according to law:
(1) failing to perform the statutory duties of social insurance;
(2) failing to deposit social insurance funds into the financial special account;
(3) docking or refusing to pay social insurance benefits on time;
(4) losing or altering social insurance data or personal rights records such as contribution records and records of social insurance benefits received; or
(5) committing other acts that violate the social insurance laws and regulations.
